# How many machinery entry points a program can CALL, and a digest of which.
#
# The register in vm_forbidden.golden counts what a program can reach and
# WRITE, and answers none. That is true and it is not the whole question. An
# operator is not a writable object, so a sealed, read-only object graph
# answers none while an operator stands that installs a replacement for any
# part of it. Calling such an operator reaches what writing the object would
# have reached, by a route the writable count cannot see.
#
# The roots are the ones a program actually holds: systemdict, userdict and
# globaldict, which need nothing, and internaldict, whose password the
# specification publishes (PLRM 8). internaldict is therefore a root a program
# holds, not a place where things are out of reach: a name moved into it is as
# callable as it was, by two tokens any program can write.
#
# THERE ARE NO NAMES HERE, AND THERE MUST NOT BE. A list of the entry points a
# program can still call is a map for whoever would like to call them, and this
# tree and its build logs are published. The count says which way the set moved
# and the digest says whether it moved at all. Someone working on it adds a
# print to the walk in tests/machinery_reachable_test.ps locally.
#
# Each count is a RATCHET: it may fall, it may not rise, and the pair is
# rewritten in the commit that moves it.
#
# nopassword -- reachable from systemdict, userdict and globaldict in a
# sandboxed run, which is the shipped default. None: the private dictionary
# and the scratch dictionary went the way of the rest, into the machinery's
# own dictionary, which the published password no longer opens. A run taking
# a census keeps them in systemdict, because its suites name them as they
# stand in the sources.
nopassword 0 0
#
# nosandbox -- the same walk in a run started with --no-sandbox. The four are
# not residue: they are the interface by which a run completes its own
# lockdown. --no-sandbox does not skip the lockdown -- the language is sealed,
# the dictionaries are split and the machinery is hidden in that run exactly
# as in any other -- it leaves the lockdown UNFINISHED, so that an embedder
# can name the paths a job may read and write and then call .lockdown itself.
# That call engages path control and removes all four, itself included.
# MEASURED: before the call `getenv` answers and the four are present; after
# it `getenv` raises invalidaccess and none of them is there.
#
# So the count is a ratchet on a deliberate interface rather than on an
# oversight, and it is held on a line of its own because these four are absent
# from a sandboxed run by configuration, not by construction: a register that
# recorded only the sandboxed run would report that absence as a property of
# the tree.
nosandbox 4 27952
#
# password -- everything internaldict holds, counted whole rather than by the
# dot the tree marks machinery with, because a machinery name carrying no dot
# is exactly the one a census keyed on dots would not see. None: what the
# operator answers with is a dictionary of its own that holds nothing, and the
# machinery's is a separate object reached only through the reference frozen
# into the bodies that use it.
password 0 0
#
# nonplrm -- names systemdict holds that PLRM 8.2 does not define, counted by
# subtracting tests/plrm-operators. This is the line that sees what the others
# cannot: the two counts above key on the leading dot, and a machinery name
# spelled without one is exactly what a dot-keyed census does not see.
# graphicsdict and DEVICE were both spelled without, and each answered with
# live machinery -- the graphics state, whose currgstate a program could
# write and whose device slot decides where the marks go, and the device
# instance itself. Both are swept now; a run taking a census keeps them,
# because its suites name them as the sources do.
#
# What is left are names the language has by convention rather than by the
# specification: the page sizes, the custom-colour and separation calls, the
# overprint and raster-operation pairs, the distiller parameters and pdfmark,
# the print shorthands, getenv and putenv, the error names, and the seven
# device loaders. Each is reachable in one token, which is right for a name a
# program is meant to call and wrong for anything else, so the count is a
# ratchet like the rest: it may fall and it may not rise.
#
# Two of them, getenv and putenv, are refused outright once the sandbox is
# engaged, so a shipped sandboxed run answers nothing through them. They are
# counted all the same: what is held here is what a program can name, and a
# name that refuses today is a name that stops refusing if its guard moves.
nonplrm 42 3884063071
